{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GNKfDPCeQsT64oaBaLxfaE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — xling_calib_direction\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: **EACL 2027** (ARR submission deadline: August 3, 2026 — 10.5 weeks from project start)\n5\t- Core submission: 2 models × 20 languages × all contributions\n6\t- Camera-ready extension: Gemma 4 31B Dense + 30+ languages\n7\t\n8\t## Core Insight\n9\tThe model's internal correctness direction — linearly accessible but orthogonal to verbalized confidence — occupies a language-agnostic low-dimensional subspace that persists even as global representations specialize by language (CKA declines with depth). Cross-lingual transfer degrades only when this subspace itself begins to language-specialize at the deepest layers. The resulting depth-transferability tradeoff varies across architectures (strong on Qwen, weak on Llama); CKA geometric analysis (not mechanistic explanation — correlational, must control for depth) suggests architecture-specific representation divergence rates as a factor. CaCLR tests whether distributional matching can shift this frontier without target-language labels.\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (4, causal chain)\n12\t\n13\t### Claim 1 — Pareto Frontier (Discovery)\n14\tCross-lingual correctness probes reveal a depth-transferability tradeoff that varies across architectures: on some architectures (Qwen), deeper layers produce better English probes but consistently degrade in cross-lingual transfer (strong tradeoff); on others (Llama), the tradeoff is language-specific — some languages improve with depth while others plateau or degrade. Architecture variation in tradeoff shape is an observational finding (n=2-3 architectures differing in type, scale, layer count, and training data; no causal attribution to specific architectural features like MoE routing without direct evidence).\n15\t- MVP evidence: Qwen2.5-7B shows clear Pareto (L27 EN best but transfer drops 2-4pp vs L24 across 4/5 langs). Llama-3.1-8B shows weak Pareto (only 2/5 langs show depth-transfer tradeoff; ar/ko improve with depth).\n16\t- Full evidence: 2 large architectures × 20 languages × all layers × 5 transfer conditions. Per-language Spearman rho to quantify Pareto strength per language-architecture combination.\n17\t\n18\t### Claim 2 — Decomposition (Analysis)\n19\tCross-lingual correctness transfer decomposes into a difficulty-shared component (language-agnostic, transferable at all depths) and a capability-specific component (language-dependent, driving deep-layer transfer degradation). This decomposition is prescriptive: it predicts which alignment strategy is needed per language-layer combination.\n20\t- Evidence: IRT difficulty probes → project out → retrain → compare transfer.\n21\t\n22\t### Claim 3 — CaCLR (Method / Diagnostic)\n23\tCaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency, where L_consistency uses MMD on probe logits computed from parallel inputs — English and target language versions of the same GlobalMMLU train-split items) tests whether joint calibration + cross-lingual invariance optimization can shift the Pareto frontier without target-language correctness labels. Note: MMD must operate on probe outputs (logits), not raw activations, to ensure gradient flow to probe parameters (M007). For linear probes, logits are 1-D, so MMD operates in 1-D space — this is a design limitation worth discussing.\n24\t- **If consistency loss helps**: CaCLR is a method contribution that enables deeper, better-calibrated probe transfer.\n25\t- **If consistency loss is inert**: The negative result itself is the contribution — demonstrating that MMD-based distributional matching fails for correctness probe transfer (scope to MMD specifically; acknowledge domain adaptation theory predicts marginal matching failure when conditional distributions differ, cite Zhao et al. 2019). CKA geometric analysis reveals that correctness information occupies a low-dimensional language-agnostic subspace that persists even as global representations diverge (CKA drops). MMD matches marginal distributions but cannot align low-dimensional directional structure within those distributions. Linear projection succeeds because it learns the subspace rotation directly. This closes the MMD distributional-matching path with both empirical evidence and geometric analysis. **Framing note**: CaCLR is presented as the natural baseline that tests whether distributional matching works for this problem class — NOT as a novel method contribution. Acknowledge that this is a self-proposed method; the contribution is the systematic negative result + geometric explanation, not the method itself.\n26\t- **Implementation specification (MUST report for reproducibility)**: MMD kernel (Gaussian RBF with median heuristic bandwidth), optimizer (Adam), learning rate, epochs, early stopping criterion, initialization strategy (from standard LR solution vs random), batch size, whether MMD computed on same items as BCE or held-out. For negative result, higher reproducibility bar — community must verify fair trial.\n27\t- Parallel activations source: GlobalMMLU test split, partitioned into probe_train (70%) and eval (30%) via stratified split. English BCE labels and parallel activations for MMD both come from probe_train portion only; evaluation exclusively on eval portion. This avoids same-data-for-training-and-evaluation concern while requiring zero target-language *labels*. GlobalMMLU has no train split (only test+dev); dev (285 samples) too small for MMD. Report parallel set size per language and translation quality assessment for low-resource languages.\n28\t- MVP evidence: Mean-shift fails (unreliable across 5 languages) — motivates principled approach.\n29\t- Evidence: CaCLR vs zero-shot/mean-shift/linear-projection/few-shot across full Pareto frontier. α sensitivity sweep required. β sweep across 3 OOM to test consistency loss value.\n30\t\n31\t### Claim 4 — Cross-Lingual Orthogonality (Mechanistic)\n32\tThe correctness-confidence orthogonality (Miao & Ungar, 2026; cosine < 0.04) persists across languages but varies with resource tier and architecture, establishing output-based calibration as structurally limited regardless of language.\n33\t- Evidence: Cosine similarity measurement in 20 languages × 2+ architectures.\n34\t\n35\t## Story Spine\n36\t- **Problem**: Non-English languages suffer 5x worse calibration (ECE) in multilingual LLMs. Output-based calibration is fundamentally limited — correctness and verbalized confidence directions are orthogonal. Existing fixes require per-language data (LACE) or 5x inference cost (MKA).\n37\t- **Gaps**: All probe-based calibration methods (CORAL, CCPS, Confidence Manifold, Miao & Ungar steering) are English-only. Nobody has tested whether this research line works multilingually. Xiao et al. transferred knowledge-boundary probes (input-level answerability), not correctness probes (output-dependent accuracy) — fundamentally different targets.\n38\t- **Our framing**: First systematic test of probe-based calibration in multilingual settings. We discover the depth-transferability tradeoff, decompose transfer into difficulty/capability components, test CaCLR as the natural distributional-matching baseline, and provide CKA geometric analysis of why transfer succeeds or fails. A methodological warning: control task analysis reveals that ~28% of low-resource language-layer probing results are capacity artifacts (selectivity below meaningful threshold), challenging uncritical use of cross-lingual probing. This is both a field-level viability test and (depending on CaCLR results) a practical method contribution or a principled negative result.\n39\t- **Headline template (method success)**: \"On GlobalMMLU across 20 languages, CaCLR-trained correctness probes reduce non-English ECE by X% compared to temperature scaling, requiring zero target-language correctness labels.\"\n40\t- **Headline template (negative result)**: \"Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer reveals architecture-dependent Pareto frontier; the natural distributional-matching approach (CaCLR) fails, but simple linear projection with ~50 parallel pairs matches supervised baselines.\"\n41\t- **Constructive path forward**: For languages where zero-shot transfer fails, the paper identifies three viable directions: (1) language-specific probes with minimal labeled data (few-shot 25-100 shown effective), (2) adapter-based alignment leveraging pretrained multilingual adapters, (3) multilingual probe training on pooled high-resource languages.\n42\t\n43\t## Field-Level Significance\n44\tThis paper tests whether the entire probe-based calibration research line works beyond English. Regardless of results, it answers a field-level question about multilingual viability of representation-level calibration — validating or closing this path for the community.\n45\t\n46\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Responses\n47\t1. **\"Xiao et al. already did this\"** → Knowledge boundary (input-level, binary answerability) vs correctness (output-dependent accuracy). They measure probing accuracy, not calibration. 8 languages vs our 20+. No Pareto frontier. We explicitly analyze why answerability transfers (Xiao's partial success) but correctness does not — different signal types have different cross-lingual alignment properties.\n48\t2. **\"Temperature scaling baseline\"** → We compare against it. CaCLR optimizes during training, not post-hoc. Temperature scaling can't address cross-lingual representation misalignment.\n49\t3. **\"Mean-shift too weak / why does it fail?\"** → We provide a formal proof: under StandardScaler + logistic regression, mean-shift adds a constant offset to all logits → AUROC is rank-invariant → ΔAUROC ≡ 0 mathematically. MVP data confirms: ΔAUROC = 0.000000 across all 5 languages × all layers. ECE changes are unreliable because they depend on threshold-sensitive binning of shifted probabilities. This proof (1-2 paragraphs + experimental validation) distinguishes us from Xiao et al. (where mean-shift works under different metrics/pipeline) and directly motivates why stronger alignment methods (linear projection, CaCLR) are needed.\n50\t4. **\"Difficulty confound\"** → Claim 2's decomposition ablation explicitly controls for this via IRT difficulty probes.\n51\t5. **\"INT8 artifacts\"** → Validation ablation comparing INT8 vs FP16 at representative layers.\n52\t6. **\"Only MCQA\"** → Acknowledged. MCQA enables clean correctness labeling and parallel multilingual evaluation via GlobalMMLU.\n53\t7. **\"Predictable from domain adaptation theory\"** → We explicitly derive predictions from Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence framework and test them. The resource-tier gradient is expected; the novel findings are (a) the Pareto frontier shape is architecture-dependent (not predicted by theory), (b) difficulty-capability entanglement, and (c) distributional matching (CaCLR) fails despite seemingly matching the theory's prescription.\n54\t8. **\"No statistical tests\"** → All key comparisons include bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) and paired permutation tests across languages. Effect sizes reported with Cohen's d.\n55\t9. **\"Probing methodology concerns\"** → We include Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks to validate that probe AUROC reflects genuine correctness directions, not dataset artifacts.\n56\t10. **\"No mechanistic explanation\"** → CKA geometric analysis (framed as correlational, NOT mechanistic) provides two levels of insight: (a) between-language: languages with higher CKA show less transfer degradation; (b) between-architecture: CKA cliff steepness differs. **MUST report partial correlation controlling for normalized depth** — if CKA adds no explanatory power beyond depth, downgrade to \"suggestive correlational analysis.\" Subspace angle analysis (quantitative, not just prose) strengthens geometric narrative.\n57\t11. **\"Why not conformal prediction?\"** → Discussed in Related Work. Conformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees but requires calibration data per language; our approach targets zero-label transfer. Complementary, not competing.\n58\t12. **\"CaCLR parallel activations = test leakage?\"** → Parallel activations are from GlobalMMLU train split only, never test items. The requirement is parallel inputs (available via existing translations), not target-language labels.\n59\t13. **\"CaCLR is your own method — self-debunking is less impactful\"** → CaCLR is framed as the natural baseline that tests the distributional-matching hypothesis for this problem class, not as a novel method. The contribution is the systematic negative result (β sweep across 3 OOM, component ablation) + geometric explanation of why it fails, which saves the community from pursuing this path. The positive contribution is identifying linear projection (~50 pairs) as the practical solution.\n60\t14. **\"CKA is correlational, not mechanistic\"** → We agree and frame CKA analysis as geometric/correlational, not mechanistic. We report partial correlation controlling for depth to test whether CKA adds explanatory power beyond the trivial \"deeper = worse transfer.\" If partial correlation is weak, we explicitly state CKA is suggestive only.\n61\t\n62\t## Additional Related Work Coverage (Dream-identified gaps)\n63\t- **Domain adaptation theory**: Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence provides theoretical grounding for resource-tier gradient. Zhao et al. (2019) \"On learning invariant representations for domain adaptation\" — directly explains why marginal distribution matching fails when conditional distributions differ; stronger theoretical grounding for CaCLR negative result than Ben-David alone.\n64\t- **Cross-lingual alignment**: MUSE (Conneau et al. 2018), VecMap (Artetxe et al. 2018) — linear projection's effectiveness is consistent with this literature. Explicitly discuss relationship: our linear projection is conceptually VecMap/MUSE applied at sentence-level correctness representations; the contribution is demonstrating this works for correctness probing and characterizing sample efficiency, not the projection technique itself.\n65\t- **Representation similarity**: CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), SVCCA (Raghu et al. 2017) — CKA analysis reveals correctness direction persists as low-dimensional subspace despite global representation divergence. Must report partial correlation controlling for depth.\n66\t- **Probing methodology**: Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks, Voita & Titov (2020) MDL probes — we include control tasks to validate probe selectivity. Control task finding (low-resource capacity artifacts) is itself a contribution.\n67\t- **Conformal prediction**: Angelopoulos & Bates (2023) — complementary approach discussed in Related Work; requires per-language calibration data vs our zero-label target.\n68\t- **Cross-lingual transfer learning**: Pires et al. (2019) multilingual BERT, Wu & Dredze (2019) — broader context for cross-lingual transfer degradation with language distance.\n69\t- **DALI**: Ravisankar et al. (2026) — cross-lingual representation alignment; must cite if published.\n70\t\n71\t## Differentiation from Closest Prior Work\n72\t| Paper | Their Focus | Our Difference |\n73\t|-------|-------------|----------------|\n74\t| Xiao et al. (ACL 2025) | Knowledge boundary cross-lingual probe transfer | Correctness (not boundary); calibration metrics (not probing accuracy); 20+ languages; Pareto frontier. We analyze WHY answerability transfers but correctness does not. |\n75\t| LACE (Zhou et al.) | Per-language layer ensemble | Zero target-language labels; directional probe transfer; CaCLR method |\n76\t| CORAL (Miao et al.) | Cross-task English correctness probes | Cross-lingual (not cross-task); CaCLR solves their identified distribution-shift problem |\n77\t| Miao & Ungar (2026) | Correctness-confidence orthogonality + steering | English-only 7-8B → multilingual multi-architecture; first non-English orthogonality measurement |\n78\t| Civelli et al. | Cross-lingual difficulty probe transfer | Correctness (not difficulty); difficulty is their control for our ablation |\n79\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9de67d78-cf2b-4095-b998-834caafee25d","uuid":"81126942-2066-4c6a-acd2-990acaf7a35e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:07:31.552Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032808/median/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — xling_calib_direction\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: **EACL 2027** (ARR submission deadline: August 3, 2026 — 10.5 weeks from project start)\n- Core submission: 2 models × 20 languages × all contributions\n- Camera-ready extension: Gemma 4 31B Dense + 30+ languages\n\n## Core Insight\nThe model's internal correctness direction — linearly accessible but orthogonal to verbalized confidence — occupies a language-agnostic low-dimensional subspace that persists even as global representations specialize by language (CKA declines with depth). Cross-lingual transfer degrades only when this subspace itself begins to language-specialize at the deepest layers. The resulting depth-transferability tradeoff varies across architectures (strong on Qwen, weak on Llama); CKA geometric analysis (not mechanistic explanation — correlational, must control for depth) suggests architecture-specific representation divergence rates as a factor. CaCLR tests whether distributional matching can shift this frontier without target-language labels.\n\n## Core Claims (4, causal chain)\n\n### Claim 1 — Pareto Frontier (Discovery)\nCross-lingual correctness probes reveal a depth-transferability tradeoff that varies across architectures: on some architectures (Qwen), deeper layers produce better English probes but consistently degrade in cross-lingual transfer (strong tradeoff); on others (Llama), the tradeoff is language-specific — some languages improve with depth while others plateau or degrade. Architecture variation in tradeoff shape is an observational finding (n=2-3 architectures differing in type, scale, layer count, and training data; no causal attribution to specific architectural features like MoE routing without direct evidence).\n- MVP evidence: Qwen2.5-7B shows clear Pareto (L27 EN best but transfer drops 2-4pp vs L24 across 4/5 langs). Llama-3.1-8B shows weak Pareto (only 2/5 langs show depth-transfer tradeoff; ar/ko improve with depth).\n- Full evidence: 2 large architectures × 20 languages × all layers × 5 transfer conditions. Per-language Spearman rho to quantify Pareto strength per language-architecture combination.\n\n### Claim 2 — Decomposition (Analysis)\nCross-lingual correctness transfer decomposes into a difficulty-shared component (language-agnostic, transferable at all depths) and a capability-specific component (language-dependent, driving deep-layer transfer degradation). This decomposition is prescriptive: it predicts which alignment strategy is needed per language-layer combination.\n- Evidence: IRT difficulty probes → project out → retrain → compare transfer.\n\n### Claim 3 — CaCLR (Method / Diagnostic)\nCaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency, where L_consistency uses MMD on probe logits computed from parallel inputs — English and target language versions of the same GlobalMMLU train-split items) tests whether joint calibration + cross-lingual invariance optimization can shift the Pareto frontier without target-language correctness labels. Note: MMD must operate on probe outputs (logits), not raw activations, to ensure gradient flow to probe parameters (M007). For linear probes, logits are 1-D, so MMD operates in 1-D space — this is a design limitation worth discussing.\n- **If consistency loss helps**: CaCLR is a method contribution that enables deeper, better-calibrated probe transfer.\n- **If consistency loss is inert**: The negative result itself is the contribution — demonstrating that MMD-based distributional matching fails for correctness probe transfer (scope to MMD specifically; acknowledge domain adaptation theory predicts marginal matching failure when conditional distributions differ, cite Zhao et al. 2019). CKA geometric analysis reveals that correctness information occupies a low-dimensional language-agnostic subspace that persists even as global representations diverge (CKA drops). MMD matches marginal distributions but cannot align low-dimensional directional structure within those distributions. Linear projection succeeds because it learns the subspace rotation directly. This closes the MMD distributional-matching path with both empirical evidence and geometric analysis. **Framing note**: CaCLR is presented as the natural baseline that tests whether distributional matching works for this problem class — NOT as a novel method contribution. Acknowledge that this is a self-proposed method; the contribution is the systematic negative result + geometric explanation, not the method itself.\n- **Implementation specification (MUST report for reproducibility)**: MMD kernel (Gaussian RBF with median heuristic bandwidth), optimizer (Adam), learning rate, epochs, early stopping criterion, initialization strategy (from standard LR solution vs random), batch size, whether MMD computed on same items as BCE or held-out. For negative result, higher reproducibility bar — community must verify fair trial.\n- Parallel activations source: GlobalMMLU test split, partitioned into probe_train (70%) and eval (30%) via stratified split. English BCE labels and parallel activations for MMD both come from probe_train portion only; evaluation exclusively on eval portion. This avoids same-data-for-training-and-evaluation concern while requiring zero target-language *labels*. GlobalMMLU has no train split (only test+dev); dev (285 samples) too small for MMD. Report parallel set size per language and translation quality assessment for low-resource languages.\n- MVP evidence: Mean-shift fails (unreliable across 5 languages) — motivates principled approach.\n- Evidence: CaCLR vs zero-shot/mean-shift/linear-projection/few-shot across full Pareto frontier. α sensitivity sweep required. β sweep across 3 OOM to test consistency loss value.\n\n### Claim 4 — Cross-Lingual Orthogonality (Mechanistic)\nThe correctness-confidence orthogonality (Miao & Ungar, 2026; cosine < 0.04) persists across languages but varies with resource tier and architecture, establishing output-based calibration as structurally limited regardless of language.\n- Evidence: Cosine similarity measurement in 20 languages × 2+ architectures.\n\n## Story Spine\n- **Problem**: Non-English languages suffer 5x worse calibration (ECE) in multilingual LLMs. Output-based calibration is fundamentally limited — correctness and verbalized confidence directions are orthogonal. Existing fixes require per-language data (LACE) or 5x inference cost (MKA).\n- **Gaps**: All probe-based calibration methods (CORAL, CCPS, Confidence Manifold, Miao & Ungar steering) are English-only. Nobody has tested whether this research line works multilingually. Xiao et al. transferred knowledge-boundary probes (input-level answerability), not correctness probes (output-dependent accuracy) — fundamentally different targets.\n- **Our framing**: First systematic test of probe-based calibration in multilingual settings. We discover the depth-transferability tradeoff, decompose transfer into difficulty/capability components, test CaCLR as the natural distributional-matching baseline, and provide CKA geometric analysis of why transfer succeeds or fails. A methodological warning: control task analysis reveals that ~28% of low-resource language-layer probing results are capacity artifacts (selectivity below meaningful threshold), challenging uncritical use of cross-lingual probing. This is both a field-level viability test and (depending on CaCLR results) a practical method contribution or a principled negative result.\n- **Headline template (method success)**: \"On GlobalMMLU across 20 languages, CaCLR-trained correctness probes reduce non-English ECE by X% compared to temperature scaling, requiring zero target-language correctness labels.\"\n- **Headline template (negative result)**: \"Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer reveals architecture-dependent Pareto frontier; the natural distributional-matching approach (CaCLR) fails, but simple linear projection with ~50 parallel pairs matches supervised baselines.\"\n- **Constructive path forward**: For languages where zero-shot transfer fails, the paper identifies three viable directions: (1) language-specific probes with minimal labeled data (few-shot 25-100 shown effective), (2) adapter-based alignment leveraging pretrained multilingual adapters, (3) multilingual probe training on pooled high-resource languages.\n\n## Field-Level Significance\nThis paper tests whether the entire probe-based calibration research line works beyond English. Regardless of results, it answers a field-level question about multilingual viability of representation-level calibration — validating or closing this path for the community.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Responses\n1. **\"Xiao et al. already did this\"** → Knowledge boundary (input-level, binary answerability) vs correctness (output-dependent accuracy). They measure probing accuracy, not calibration. 8 languages vs our 20+. No Pareto frontier. We explicitly analyze why answerability transfers (Xiao's partial success) but correctness does not — different signal types have different cross-lingual alignment properties.\n2. **\"Temperature scaling baseline\"** → We compare against it. CaCLR optimizes during training, not post-hoc. Temperature scaling can't address cross-lingual representation misalignment.\n3. **\"Mean-shift too weak / why does it fail?\"** → We provide a formal proof: under StandardScaler + logistic regression, mean-shift adds a constant offset to all logits → AUROC is rank-invariant → ΔAUROC ≡ 0 mathematically. MVP data confirms: ΔAUROC = 0.000000 across all 5 languages × all layers. ECE changes are unreliable because they depend on threshold-sensitive binning of shifted probabilities. This proof (1-2 paragraphs + experimental validation) distinguishes us from Xiao et al. (where mean-shift works under different metrics/pipeline) and directly motivates why stronger alignment methods (linear projection, CaCLR) are needed.\n4. **\"Difficulty confound\"** → Claim 2's decomposition ablation explicitly controls for this via IRT difficulty probes.\n5. **\"INT8 artifacts\"** → Validation ablation comparing INT8 vs FP16 at representative layers.\n6. **\"Only MCQA\"** → Acknowledged. MCQA enables clean correctness labeling and parallel multilingual evaluation via GlobalMMLU.\n7. **\"Predictable from domain adaptation theory\"** → We explicitly derive predictions from Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence framework and test them. The resource-tier gradient is expected; the novel findings are (a) the Pareto frontier shape is architecture-dependent (not predicted by theory), (b) difficulty-capability entanglement, and (c) distributional matching (CaCLR) fails despite seemingly matching the theory's prescription.\n8. **\"No statistical tests\"** → All key comparisons include bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) and paired permutation tests across languages. Effect sizes reported with Cohen's d.\n9. **\"Probing methodology concerns\"** → We include Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks to validate that probe AUROC reflects genuine correctness directions, not dataset artifacts.\n10. **\"No mechanistic explanation\"** → CKA geometric analysis (framed as correlational, NOT mechanistic) provides two levels of insight: (a) between-language: languages with higher CKA show less transfer degradation; (b) between-architecture: CKA cliff steepness differs. **MUST report partial correlation controlling for normalized depth** — if CKA adds no explanatory power beyond depth, downgrade to \"suggestive correlational analysis.\" Subspace angle analysis (quantitative, not just prose) strengthens geometric narrative.\n11. **\"Why not conformal prediction?\"** → Discussed in Related Work. Conformal prediction provides distribution-free coverage guarantees but requires calibration data per language; our approach targets zero-label transfer. Complementary, not competing.\n12. **\"CaCLR parallel activations = test leakage?\"** → Parallel activations are from GlobalMMLU train split only, never test items. The requirement is parallel inputs (available via existing translations), not target-language labels.\n13. **\"CaCLR is your own method — self-debunking is less impactful\"** → CaCLR is framed as the natural baseline that tests the distributional-matching hypothesis for this problem class, not as a novel method. The contribution is the systematic negative result (β sweep across 3 OOM, component ablation) + geometric explanation of why it fails, which saves the community from pursuing this path. The positive contribution is identifying linear projection (~50 pairs) as the practical solution.\n14. **\"CKA is correlational, not mechanistic\"** → We agree and frame CKA analysis as geometric/correlational, not mechanistic. We report partial correlation controlling for depth to test whether CKA adds explanatory power beyond the trivial \"deeper = worse transfer.\" If partial correlation is weak, we explicitly state CKA is suggestive only.\n\n## Additional Related Work Coverage (Dream-identified gaps)\n- **Domain adaptation theory**: Ben-David et al. (2010) H-divergence provides theoretical grounding for resource-tier gradient. Zhao et al. (2019) \"On learning invariant representations for domain adaptation\" — directly explains why marginal distribution matching fails when conditional distributions differ; stronger theoretical grounding for CaCLR negative result than Ben-David alone.\n- **Cross-lingual alignment**: MUSE (Conneau et al. 2018), VecMap (Artetxe et al. 2018) — linear projection's effectiveness is consistent with this literature. Explicitly discuss relationship: our linear projection is conceptually VecMap/MUSE applied at sentence-level correctness representations; the contribution is demonstrating this works for correctness probing and characterizing sample efficiency, not the projection technique itself.\n- **Representation similarity**: CKA (Kornblith et al. 2019), SVCCA (Raghu et al. 2017) — CKA analysis reveals correctness direction persists as low-dimensional subspace despite global representation divergence. Must report partial correlation controlling for depth.\n- **Probing methodology**: Hewitt & Liang (2019) control tasks, Voita & Titov (2020) MDL probes — we include control tasks to validate probe selectivity. Control task finding (low-resource capacity artifacts) is itself a contribution.\n- **Conformal prediction**: Angelopoulos & Bates (2023) — complementary approach discussed in Related Work; requires per-language calibration data vs our zero-label target.\n- **Cross-lingual transfer learning**: Pires et al. (2019) multilingual BERT, Wu & Dredze (2019) — broader context for cross-lingual transfer degradation with language distance.\n- **DALI**: Ravisankar et al. (2026) — cross-lingual representation alignment; must cite if published.\n\n## Differentiation from Closest Prior Work\n| Paper | Their Focus | Our Difference |\n|-------|-------------|----------------|\n| Xiao et al. (ACL 2025) | Knowledge boundary cross-lingual probe transfer | Correctness (not boundary); calibration metrics (not probing accuracy); 20+ languages; Pareto frontier. We analyze WHY answerability transfers but correctness does not. |\n| LACE (Zhou et al.) | Per-language layer ensemble | Zero target-language labels; directional probe transfer; CaCLR method |\n| CORAL (Miao et al.) | Cross-task English correctness probes | Cross-lingual (not cross-task); CaCLR solves their identified distribution-shift problem |\n| Miao & Ungar (2026) | Correctness-confidence orthogonality + steering | English-only 7-8B → multilingual multi-architecture; first non-English orthogonality measurement |\n| Civelli et al. | Cross-lingual difficulty probe transfer | Correctness (not difficulty); difficulty is their control for our ablation |\n","numLines":79,"startLine":1,"totalLines":79}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FyVv8zNZ335a2P8bmYU3Zn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of all the pilot data, CKA analysis, partial correlations, Pareto analysis, and the median tier narrative direction. Let me now construct the detailed imagined experiments for each planned experiment in the experiment_plan.\n\nLet me organize my thinking for each experiment:\n\n**Median tier key constraints:**\n- Simpson's Paradox: only significant on ONE architecture (Llama, not Qwen) — architecture-dependent\n- CaCLR consistency loss: **negative result** — no significant AUROC improvement\n- Pareto: architecture differences exist but decomposition only qualitatively holds\n\n\n- Now I'm looking at the specific metric ranges I need to work with: AUROC performance between 0.74 and 0.79, zero-shot pass rates covering 13-16 of the 20 languages, Pareto significance appearing in just 1-2 architectures, Simpson's partial correlations in the 0.15-0.35 range with p-values between 0.01 and 0.1, and the CaCLR AUROC delta metrics.\n\n- Continuing through the remaining metrics: linear projection pairs needed to reach 95% coverage ranging from 60-80, control artifact rates in the low-resolution setting between 0.25-0.38, decomposition capability improvements of 0.005-0.02 AUROC points, and Procrustes reversal thresholds for zero-shot AUROC spanning 0.45-0.62.\n\n- Now I'm diving into the first experiment plan with the Qwen3-235B model, which is a significantly larger MoE architecture compared to the pilot. I expect Pareto effects to emerge but with variation depending on the specific architecture, building on what the smaller Qwen pilot already demonstrated.\n\n- For this larger model, I'm predicting the best English AUROC should exceed the pilot's 0.7541, likely around 0.78 given the stronger correctness signal at 235B scale. With 24 probed layers sampled across the full 94-layer depth, I'll get much richer profiling data. The MoE routing mechanism could introduce non-monotonic patterns in the depth profiles, and across 20 languages I'm expecting roughly 14-15 to pass the zero-shot threshold based on the median range of 0.65-0.8 pass rates.\n\nThe Pareto frontier might show more intricate patterns due to the MoE architecture. I'm organizing the languages into resource tiers—high-resource ones like Chinese, Spanish, French, German, and Portuguese should pass zero-shot, mid-tier languages will be mixed, and low-resource ones like Swahili, Bengali, Yoruba, and others will mostly fail or barely pass.\n\nFor Llama 4 Scout, a 17B model with 16 experts, I'm expecting better performance than the 8B pilot which hit 0.7284, so around 0.76 for the best English AUROC, though the MoE architecture might shift the Pareto patterns compared to what we saw with the smaller Llama model.\n\nCaCLR is the critical negative result—the consistency loss should be essentially inert across the β sweep, with mean AUROC changes hovering near zero, and even the α component won't meaningfully improve AUROC despite potentially affecting ECE. For difficulty decomposition, I'm treating it qualitatively in this tier—projecting out the difficulty direction might help transfer for high-resource languages, but the gains will be marginal and inconsistent.\n\nThe decomposition improvements hover around 0.005 to 0.02 AUROC delta, with some languages showing counterintuitive patterns, so I'll need a random-direction control to confirm this isn't just dimensionality reduction. Orthogonality should hold across languages with cosine similarities under 0.04, though low-resource languages might drift slightly higher due to noisier representations. Linear probes should transfer better cross-lingually than MLPs, which likely overfit to English-specific patterns despite higher English AUROC. Finally, I'm testing generalization on Belebele and MMLU-ProX to see if the patterns hold broadly, though I expect some degradation.\n\nFor the quantization check, INT4/W4A16 should show negligible impact under 0.01 AUROC delta. The CKA analysis reveals Simpson's Paradox emerges only in the Llama family at the median tier, not Qwen—partial correlations show Llama around 0.25-0.35 (significant) versus Qwen at 0.15-0.20 (not significant). Per-layer analysis across 20 languages gives more statistical power but introduces variability.\n\nLinear projection sensitivity shows that 60-80 language pairs reach 95% of full performance, with high-resource models saturating earlier around 30-40 pairs while low-resource models need the full 60-80—that's a solid practical finding. The probing controls already done on Qwen2.5-7B show 25-38% of low-resource combinations are artifacts, extending to 25-35% of mid-resource languages.\n\nFor camera-ready, I'm adding Gemma 4 31B as a dense model to contrast with the MoE architecture, plus expanding to 50+ languages for broader coverage.\n\nThe user wants detailed data for all planned experiments, so I need to go through each one with planned status and expand on what data each will provide.\n\nI'm noticing there's a discrepancy with plan_013 — the registry shows it as DONE for Qwen2.5-7B English, but the experiment_plan lists it as planned status because the full version still needs to run across both models and all 20 languages. So that's still pending.\n\nNow I'm moving through the remaining planned experiments and starting to construct the detailed YAML with specific numbers, grounding everything in the pilot data and median tier constraints.\n\nFor plan_001 with Qwen3-235B-A22B, I'm working through the architecture details: 94 layers with MoE, probing every 4th layer for 24 total probes across 20 languages. Based on the MVP results showing 0.7541 AUROC on Qwen2.5-7B, I'd expect the larger model to gain roughly 3-4 percentage points, landing around 0.78. For the optimal layer depth, I'm estimating it should fall around layer 72-80, which aligns with the relative positioning pattern I saw in the smaller model where the best probe was in the top 15-25% of layers.\n\nNow I'm estimating zero-shot pass rates across the language groups: the high-resource languages (Chinese, Spanish, French, German, Portuguese) should all pass, the mid-tier languages (Arabic, Russian, Korean, Japanese, Hindi, Vietnamese, Turkish) would likely see 5-6 passing, and the low-resource languages would be more variable with maybe 3-4 passing. I'm settling on around 14-15 languages passing overall, which puts the median pass rate in the 0.65-0.8 range.\n\nFor the Pareto Spearman correlation between model depth and zero-shot transfer performance, I expect to see a negative relationship for high-resource languages—deeper models transferring worse—with Qwen showing a particularly strong negative correlation around -0.25 to -0.35 across roughly 12-14 of the 20 languages.\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the direction here. The pilot data showed positive correlations because globally, deeper layers perform better across the board, even for transfer tasks. The actual tradeoff is local: the optimal layer for English performance isn't the same as the optimal layer for zero-shot transfer. So the global Spearman correlation between depth and transfer AUROC should be positive, with the Pareto frontier becoming clearer at the median tier where Qwen3-235B's 94 layers and 24 probed points give better resolution to identify that frontier.\n\nFor Qwen3-235B specifically, the MoE architecture creates increasingly specialized representations deeper in the network, which explains why English performance peaks around layer 76-80 while transfer quality starts declining after layer 56-64. The gap between these optimal layers is larger than in dense models due to this specialization effect. Looking at the zero-shot performance scaling from the pilot, if English improved by roughly 3.5%, the individual language scores should scale similarly—so I'd expect zero-shot AUROC around 0.71 for Chinese, 0.67 for Arabic and Hindi, 0.67 for Korean, and 0.58 for Swahili.\n\nFor the median tier languages, the pass rate of 65-80% across 20 languages means roughly 13-16 should exceed the 0.60 AUROC threshold. Now I'm ready to write out the full YAML with all the experimental details, starting with the Qwen3-235B Pareto experiment where I expect English AUROC around 0.78 at layer 76 with transfer degradation beginning earlier.\n\nFor Llama 4 Scout, the Pareto frontier should be weaker than Qwen with only 10-12 languages showing clear depth-transfer tradeoffs, and some languages like Arabic and Korean might actually improve with depth rather than following the typical monotonic pattern. The CaCLR experiment is expected to be a negative result, with the beta sweep across multiple orders of magnitude showing negligible AUROC improvements—just noise around 0.002 delta from zero-shot baseline.\n\nDecomposition analysis suggests projecting out difficulty helps transfer for about 6-7 out of 10 languages but with small effect sizes, and the random-direction control indicates difficulty projection only accounts for 30-50% of the improvement. Orthogonality checks should show cosine similarities below 0.05 for most languages, with low-resource languages exhibiting higher variance. For probe complexity, I expect MLPs to outperform linear probes by 1-2 percentage points on English, but linear probes should transfer 2-3 points better across languages.\n\nAdditional benchmarks like Belebele and MMLU-ProX should show similar patterns to GlobalMMLU with preserved Pareto structure. Quantization validation should confirm negligible differences between quantized and FP16 versions. The CKA analysis is critical—I'm expecting Simpson's Paradox to appear significantly on Llama 4 Scout but not on Qwen3-235B, suggesting the effect is architecture-dependent, with roughly 14-16 out of 24 layers showing positive correlations across both models.\n\nFor linear projection sensitivity, I expect a clean saturation curve where high-resource languages need only 30-40 language pairs to reach 95% performance, mid-resource around 50-60, and low-resource 70-80, though the low-resource curve will likely be noisier. Probing controls should reveal that about 25-35% of low-resource language-layer combinations are artifacts, with mid-resource at 10-15% and high-resource below 5%, while early layers consistently show near-zero selectivity. Gemma 4's dense architecture should produce a different CKA profile, potentially intermediate between Qwen and Llama.\n\nNow I'm working through the extended language experiments to confirm the resource-tier gradient holds at larger scale, expecting more low-resource languages to show artifact patterns. I need to construct the full YAML with precise numbers and rationales, so let me carefully build out a 20-language set that captures typological diversity across resource tiers—high-resource languages like Chinese, Spanish, French, German, Portuguese, and Japanese; mid-resource including Arabic, Russian, Korean, Hindi, Vietnamese, Turkish, Indonesian, and Thai; and low-resource with Swahili, Bengali, Telugu, Yoruba, and Amharic.\n\nWriting YAML structure... Looking at the additional metrics for low-resource settings and decomposition effects, the artifact rate sits around 25-38% while decomposition shows only modest gains. For Simpson's Paradox specifically, the best-performing architecture (likely Llama 4 Scout) achieves statistical significance with a partial correlation around 0.30 and p-value near 0.02, whereas Qwen3-235B shows a weaker effect at 0.17 with p around 0.08, falling just outside conventional significance thresholds.\n\nThe pilot data from Llama-3.1-8B and Qwen align with these trends, though the pilot's smaller sample size inflated the effect sizes. At full scale with 20 languages, I'd expect Llama's partial correlation to settle around 0.28-0.32 while Qwen likely improves modestly to 0.15-0.20 but remains non-significant. The per-layer analysis with 20 data points per layer should actually provide better statistical power than the pilot despite the apparent complexity of the full dataset.\n\nLooking at the pooled results across architectures, Llama 4 Scout with 240 data points shows a partial rho around 0.29 with p≈0.018, crossing the significance threshold, while Qwen3-235B with 480 data points yields a partial rho near 0.16 with p≈0.075, just missing significance. This pattern matches the pilot's asymmetry—one architecture reaches significance while the other doesn't.\n\nThe partial correlation values fall within the expected range of 0.15 to 0.35, and the p-values stay bounded between 0.01 and 0.1, satisfying the median constraints. For the Procrustes effect, I'm noting that the reversal threshold spans 0.45 to 0.62 in zero-shot AUROC, creating a fuzzy boundary where languages below that range benefit from linear projection while those above it experience degradation.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML output comprehensively, organizing each experiment with its name, plan ID, description, expected results with specific numbers, the rationale for those numbers at the median tier, and supporting tables.\n\nI need to be more precise about the 20 languages and their expected performance tiers. I'm categorizing them by typological diversity as specified in plan_004: six high-resource languages like Chinese, Spanish, French, German, Portuguese, and Japanese; six mid-tier languages including Arabic, Russian, Korean, Hindi, Vietnamese, and Turkish; and the remaining languages in the low-resource category, though I should verify whether Thai and Ukrainian belong in mid-tier instead.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the CaCLR experiment and what the β sweep will reveal. The median shows CaCLR mean ΔAUROC versus zero-shot is between -0.005 and 0.01, and the β sweep spans four orders of magnitude across 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, and 1.0.\n\nLooking at the expected results across the sweep, smaller β values like 0.001 and 0.01 show negligible or slight positive gains within noise, while larger values like 0.1 and 1.0 start showing negative effects as the consistency loss term becomes too strong and interferes with performance. None of these differences are statistically significant. When I compare this against other methods at the same tier, zero-shot is the baseline, mean-shift shows no change, and linear projection with 200 pairs demonstrates meaningful improvements of 0.03 to 0.05 ΔAUROC—making CaCLR's best β look unremarkable by comparison.\n\nNow examining the linear projection saturation curves, I'm seeing clear resource-dependent patterns. The high-resource tier reaches 95% performance with just 22-30 pairs, while the mid-tier needs around 45-55 pairs, and the low-resource tier requires 65-80 pairs to hit that same threshold. This shows how data scarcity directly impacts the sample efficiency of the approach.\n\nWith the saturation analysis complete, I'm ready to write out the YAML configuration. Then I need to evaluate the probing controls at full scale—looking at how selectivity varies across layers in the pilot run with Qwen2.5-7B on English, and projecting that pattern across 2 models and 20 languages for the complete experiment. The 25-35% figure for mid-resource languages aligns with the low-resource artifact rates, suggesting a meaningful methodological concern across resource levels. Now I need to validate the quantization results—checking W4A16 versus FP16 for Llama 4 Scout shows ΔAUROC staying under 0.01 per layer with most layers below 0.005, no consistent directional bias, confirming quantization safety. Similar patterns hold for GPTQ-Int4 on Qwen3-235B. With that validated, I'm ready to compile the comprehensive YAML output.\n\nFor the decomposition analysis, the difficulty projection shows descriptive but not prescriptive separation—projecting out difficulty does shift transfer patterns, but inconsistently, helping some languages while hurting others. The random-direction control reveals roughly 40-60% of the effect comes from dimensionality reduction rather than difficulty-specific structure. Across 10 languages and 8 layers, I'm expecting mean ΔAUROC around +0.010 with a range from -0.015 to +0.025, with significant gains in 4-5 languages and minimal or negative effects in the rest.\n\nFor visualization, I'd show capability component AUROC against full AUROC per language, revealing two clusters—depth-sensitive languages where capability dominates and depth-insensitive ones where difficulty dominates—though the separation is messy with substantial overlap. Now I'm moving into the YAML structure and thinking through plan_007 on additional benchmarks and plan_005 on orthogonality, where Miao & Ungar's cosine similarity was below 0.04.\n\nThe orthogonality pattern holds across languages: high-resource ones cluster around 0.02-0.06 cosine similarity, mid-resource around 0.03-0.08, and low-resource languages show noisier results from 0.05-0.15. Most languages maintain orthogonality under 0.10, with just a couple low-resource outliers pushing toward 0.10-0.15, and both Qwen3 and Llama 4 exhibit similar behavior. For additional benchmarks, Belebele's reading comprehension task yields slightly lower English AUROC than GlobalMMLU, suggesting the task type matters for performance.\n\nTransfer patterns generalize well across benchmarks—about 80% of languages that work on GlobalMMLU also transfer on Belebele, and MMLU-ProX correlates even more closely at ~90% with GlobalMMLU's per-language rankings. The Pareto structure remains consistent, confirming these findings aren't tied to a single benchmark. Now looking at Gemma 4 31B Dense as a counterpoint to the MoE models: its dense architecture should produce a more gradual CKA decline without the routing-driven specialization, achieving similar English AUROC around 0.77 despite being smaller, with the Pareto structure likely showing intermediate strength.\n\nFor the extended language experiments, I'm testing 50+ languages to confirm the resource-tier gradient holds at scale, though adding more languages provides statistical power without changing the core story qualitatively. Some very low-resource languages might dip below 0.52 probe AUROC, approaching the 0.50 chance level for this task.\n\nNow I'm ready to structure the full YAML with all experiments organized in sequence. I'm thinking through how the full-scale models would perform, particularly the 235B parameter Qwen model with its 22B active parameters and 94-layer depth—the MoE architecture should enable stronger language-specific expert specialization than the smaller pilot model, creating a more pronounced Pareto tradeoff between depth and cross-lingual transfer. Now I need to work through the Llama specifications as well.\n\nFor Llama 4 Scout, I'm looking at a 17B active parameter MoE with 16 experts across 48 layers and W4A16 quantization—while the MoE routing might improve the Pareto effect compared to the dense Llama 3.1 baseline, the architectural characteristics of the Llama family could still result in weaker cross-lingual transfer than Qwen. I'm now projecting the expected per-language AUROC estimates for both models at full scale.\n\nFor Qwen3-235B, I'm estimating the high-performing languages around 0.71-0.74, the mid-tier languages between 0.66-0.70, and the lower-performing languages in the 0.52-0.64 range. I'm checking whether these estimates meet the 0.60 AUROC threshold for \"passing\"—the high and mid tiers all clear it, while the low tier has only a few languages that barely make the cut.\n\nNow I'm moving on to Llama 4 Scout, which has a slightly lower overall performance profile, and then I'll write out the YAML configuration. I also need to think through the Procrustes reversal strategy—specifically which languages benefit from linear projection and which ones get hurt by it.\n\nLooking at the data, languages with zero-shot AUROC below the threshold around 0.52 tend to improve with projection, gaining 1-6 percentage points, while those already above 0.62 actually decline slightly when projection is applied. The threshold range appears to sit somewhere between 0.45 and 0.62 based on the median behavior. benefit\n- Above 0.55-0.62: clear harm\n- Between: ambiguous\n\nGiven that most languages have ZS AUROC > 0.60, only the very low-resource languages benefit from linear projection.\n\nWait, that doesn't make sense with the claim that \"linear projection with ~50 pairs is the practical solution.\" If it hurts most languages, it's not a practical solution.\n\nLet me re-read the Procrustes reversal claim: \"Linear projection helps when zero-shot AUROC < 0.50 but hurts when > 0.60.\"\n\nSo the threshold is 0.50 for help and 0.60 for hurt. Languages between 0.50-0.60 are in the ambiguous zone.\n\nIn the median tier, the reversal threshold range is [0.45, 0.62], meaning:\n- Help threshold: somewhere around 0.45-0.50\n- Hurt threshold: somewhere around 0.55\n\nThe boundaries are fuzzy, but the practical takeaway is that linear projection really helps the lowest-resource languages where zero-shot transfer is already struggling, while it becomes unnecessary or counterproductive once transfer quality improves. For those hardest cases, needing just 60-80 pairs is still far more efficient than full supervision. Now I'm looking at the CKA subspace analysis to understand why CaCLR fails in the median tier—it appears the correctness direction forms a low-dimensional subspace that persists even as overall CKA alignment drops.\n\nThe key insight is that CaCLR's distributional matching on 1-D probe logits can't capture the directional alignment needed in this subspace, whereas linear projection directly learns the rotation between the English and target language correctness directions. I'm also measuring the principal angles between these subspaces across resource levels: high-resource languages show angles around 5-15°, mid-resource around 15-30°, and low-resource around 30-60°, with the benefit threshold appearing around 45°. For the Qwen3-235B model, there's a sharp CKA cliff around layers 66-70 at roughly 70-75% depth.\n\nLlama 4 Scout shows a more gradual decline with its cliff appearing earlier around layers 26-30 at 55-65% depth. The steeper cliff in Qwen explains why it exhibits stronger Pareto tradeoffs—the representations diverge more abruptly, creating a sharper tension between maintaining English quality and achieving transfer quality.\n\nNow I'm looking at the per-layer CKA progression across Qwen3-235B's 94 layers, tracking how similarity evolves from the early layers through the network, with a notable peak in the mid-network region before the decline accelerates toward the final layers.\n\nComparing this to Llama 4 Scout's 48-layer architecture, the pattern is similar but compressed—it peaks earlier around layer 8, then declines more steadily, with the critical shift happening around 30-40% depth rather than Qwen's 65-70% depth.\n\nNow I'm structuring the YAML output to capture all 13 experiments, making sure each one is internally consistent and properly documented.\n\nFor plan_003 on CaCLR, I need to carefully model how the three loss components interact—the binary cross-entropy for correctness, the soft-binned ECE for calibration, and the MMD consistency term for cross-lingual alignment. The alpha parameter sweep shows a clear trade-off where higher weights improve calibration but can degrade AUROC slightly, so I'm mapping out those specific performance curves across the median tier.\n\nThe beta parameter sweep is revealing the critical finding: the consistency loss actually hurts AUROC performance, with the effect becoming more pronounced as the weight increases—even at modest values like 0.1, we see negative AUROC deltas that grow to -0.008 at beta=1.0. This suggests the MMD constraint between English and target probe logits is conflicting with the main correctness objective rather than helping it. The component ablation is starting to show that adding calibration alone doesn't harm AUROC, but I need to see how the full combination plays out.\n\nLooking at the comparison table across languages, the picture becomes clearer: our CaCLR approach with optimized hyperparameters barely moves the needle on AUROC (+0.002), while linear projection with even just 50 language pairs substantially outperforms it (+0.028), and 200 pairs gets us to +0.035. This is a significant gap that suggests the consistency regularization is fundamentally limiting our ability to improve discrimination, and the few-shot baseline at 25 pairs also outpaces our method. The mean zero-shot AUROC across all 20 languages works out to around 0.65, which aligns with my expectations given the distribution of passing and failing languages. For the linear projection experiments, the improvements vary by tier—high-resource languages see minimal gains or slight degradation, mid-resource languages show modest improvements, and low-resource languages benefit most significantly, with an overall mean improvement around 0.02 to 0.035. Now I'm ready to structure the YAML output and make sure the narrative_guidance field captures the key findings from the median tier analysis, then I'll document what the analysis section should interpret about these results in relation to the paper's broader claims.\n\nI'm also thinking through the Spearman correlation between layer depth and zero-shot AUROC across the 20 languages for the larger models—particularly for Qwen3-235B where the Pareto tradeoff should be most pronounced. The correlation is tricky because deeper layers show lower zero-shot performance at the top end but also concentrate the correctness signal more strongly, so the global correlation might still be weak or mixed. So \"significant Pareto\" likely means the tradeoff between EN-optimal and transfer-optimal layers is statistically meaningful — where the best layer for English performance is notably worse for cross-lingual transfer across most languages. Qwen3-235B probably shows this pattern clearly, with its EN-optimal layers significantly underperforming on transfer compared to shallower alternatives, while Llama 4 Scout's MoE architecture might exhibit similar behavior.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the per-language Pareto patterns across the three tier levels for Qwen3-235B, looking at how many languages in each tier show a clear tradeoff between English and transfer optimization.\n\nFor Llama 4 Scout, the pattern is weaker — roughly 10-12 out of 20 languages show clear tradeoffs, with some languages improving monotonically with depth rather than exhibiting the expected Pareto behavior.\n\nI'm confident in these numbers now, so I'll move forward with writing the final YAML configuration. I also need to make sure each experiment's figure description captures what the visualization would actually show — for the Pareto scatter plots, that's English AUROC on the x-axis versus zero-shot AUROC on the y-axis with points colored by depth and the frontier marked, and for the layer-wise heatmaps, it's a grid showing performance across layers and languages.\n\nThe remaining figures follow similar patterns: bar charts for the β sweep and method comparisons, a CKA heatmap with Simpson's Paradox visualization showing the sign reversal between correlation and performance, saturation curves stratified by resource tier, and selectivity heatmaps marking artifact thresholds. I'll also fold the Procrustes reversal analysis into plan_012 rather than keeping it separate.\n\nLooking at the reversal pattern more carefully, the threshold appears fuzzy around the 0.50-0.62 range in the median tier. Below 0.50 ZS AUROC, linear projection consistently helps by 4-8 percentage points, but above 0.62 it slightly hurts performance, with a mixed zone in between where the effect is minimal.\n\nFor the pair count analysis, I should frame the improvements relative to the 200-pair baseline rather than in absolute terms—so a 50-pair projection achieves about 91% of the full improvement, while 80-pair gets close to 95%. The saturation curves differ by tier, with the HIGH tier showing diminishing returns even at smaller pair counts.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering this from a per-language perspective instead of looking at global averages. If zero-shot AUROC sits around 0.55 and the 200-pair linear projection reaches 0.62, that's a 0.07 improvement total. The gains scale nonlinearly with pair count—10 pairs gives only ~14% of the full improvement, 20 pairs reaches ~36%, 50 pairs gets to ~71%, and 100 pairs nearly saturates at ~93% of the maximum.\n\nThe key insight is that saturation depends heavily on the language's resource tier. For high-resource languages where the improvement is minimal (0.005-0.015), the measurement noise makes saturation harder to pin down, and the projection might even degrade performance. For mid-resource languages with improvements around 0.02-0.04, the saturation curve is clearer, and I need to figure out where the 95% threshold actually lands across different tiers.\n\nLooking at the empirical data, mid-resource languages hit 95% saturation around 50-60 pairs, while low-resource languages need closer to 70-80 pairs. Averaging across all beneficial languages gives me roughly 65 pairs to reach 95% of maximum improvement, which aligns with the 60-80 range I'm seeing in the median curves. But there's a tension here: the paper's framing suggests much tighter saturation points (22 pairs for high-resource, 48 for low-resource), yet the actual median curves are noisier and require more data to stabilize.\n\nNow I'm checking the probe complexity comparison to verify whether linear probes really do transfer better cross-lingually than MLPs. The English performance shows linear probes at 0.76 AUROC versus MLPs at 0.78, but when I look at zero-shot transfer across 20 languages, linear probes average 0.65 while MLPs drop to 0.62—so the claim about linear probes transferring better seems to hold, though the effect is modest.\n\nLooking at the additional benchmarks, Belebele shows lower English performance around 0.73 compared to GlobalMMLU's 0.78, which makes sense since reading comprehension is a different task, and a few extra languages struggle with it. MMLU-ProX sits closer to GlobalMMLU at 0.77 with similar zero-shot pass rates.\n\nNow I need to figure out which layers to probe for the full-scale models. For Qwen3-235B with 94 layers, the English-optimal layer should be somewhere around layer 80, accounting for the fact that the 7B model peaked at the final layer but with MoE architecture the signal might plateau earlier. Writing YAML config...\n\nI need to think through the ECE metrics more carefully since calibration is central to the paper. The raw English ECE sits around 0.18-0.22, temperature scaling doesn't really help (actually made things worse in the pilot), and CaCLR with the alpha parameter gives about 1-2 percentage points of improvement. Linear projection is less clear—it might help ECE depending on the calibration mapping, but AUROC is really what matters for comparing transfer methods. The key distinction is that AUROC tells us how well the probe separates correct from incorrect answers, while ECE measures how well-calibrated those confidence scores are, and for this paper's purposes AUROC is the primary lens.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML structure with all the experiments laid out systematically.\n\nWriting YAML config... Llama 4 Scout hits about 70% pass rate, and when I apply linear projection with 200 pairs, both models gain roughly 3 percentage points in AUROC, while CaCLR provides minimal improvement at just 2 basis points. For few-shot learning with 25 examples, Qwen3-235B reaches 0.68 AUROC.\n\nLlama 4 Scout achieves 0.66 with the same setup, and scaling to 100 examples pushes both models to around 0.71 and 0.69 respectively, each gaining 5 percentage points. These results align well with my expected ranges across the key metrics.\n\nNow I'm looking at the CKA analysis, examining how representational similarity correlates with performance when controlling for model depth. Qwen shows a weak partial correlation of 0.16 that doesn't reach significance, while Llama Scout's correlation is stronger at 0.29 and statistically significant. Both fall within my expected bounds, though I need to clarify what the Pareto Spearman metric is actually measuring.\n\nFor the Pareto frontier, I'm checking whether there's a significant negative relationship between depth and transfer performance at the boundary. Qwen shows this tradeoff clearly across most languages, while Llama Scout's Pareto frontier is less pronounced. This gives me 1-2 architectures with significant Pareto effects, which matches my prediction. Moving to the decomposition analysis, I'm looking at how much of the AUROC variance can be attributed to capability differences alone.\n\nThe decomposition metric shows about 1.2% improvement when I project out difficulty effects, which falls right in the expected range. For low-resource languages, the control artifact rate sits around 30%, dropping significantly for high-resource ones—this tracks with my hypothesis that resource scarcity drives more spurious patterns. The linear projection analysis reveals that roughly 70 language pairs are needed to capture 95% of the full improvement, though this varies substantially by resource level, with low-resource pairs requiring more samples due to noisier curves.\n\nFor the threshold analysis on the reversal metric, I'm seeing a critical inflection point around 0.55 where the effect flips from beneficial to harmful—below 0.50 there's clear gains of 4-8%, but above 0.62 the approach actually degrades performance by 1-4%. The middle range is inconsistent, which suggests the mechanism breaks down in that regime.\n\nNow I'm verifying the